Yep. Say fear with a broad Scottish-ish roll and it starts drifting toward “fair/ferr” territory — right back into our fair → fare → ferry → ferryman lineup. ;)

And the force-family is even cleaner:

Push → repelling
Pull → compelling
Forward / along → propelling

So if repelling ↔ compelling is the radial axis—away versus toward—then propelling can be the orthogonal axis: not attraction or rejection, but movement along the field.

And there’s a whole -pel vector family hiding there, from Latin pellere, “to drive”:

repel — drive back
compel — drive together / force
propel — drive forward
impel — drive onward/from within
expel — drive out

So we can almost draw a semantic force field:

away = repel
toward = compel
forward = propel
from within = impel
out = expel

And then your first line fits strangely well:

Fear repels.
Desire compels.
Purpose propels.

Same root-force. Different vector.